#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# parametric_plot

#% text
# `parametric_plot([fx, fy], [t, a, b])` draws the curve of points (fx, fy) as the parameter t runs from a to b.

#% text
# `parametric_plot([[fx, fy], [gx, gy], ...], [t, a, b])` draws several curves in one picture, each in its own colour.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The value is `image("path")`, a PNG file with SVG and PDF copies next to it.

#% item
# Both axes use the same unit, so a circle is drawn round; the picture takes the shape of the curves.

#% item
# The parameter is sampled adaptively: more densely where the curve bends sharply.

#% item
# Where a coordinate is not a finite real number, or the curve jumps, the line is broken.

#% item
# The options `x_label`, `y_label`, `legend`, `legend_position`, `colors`, `line_style` and `plot_range` work as for `plot`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The unit circle:

#% cell
parametric_plot([cos(t), sin(t)], [t, 0, 2pi])
#% output
#= image("parametric_plot.files/parametric_plot-1.svgz")

#% text
# A figure whose coordinates oscillate at different rates:

#% cell
parametric_plot([sin(2t), sin(3t)], [t, 0, 2pi])
#% output
#= image("parametric_plot.files/parametric_plot-2.svgz")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Two curves, named in a legend:

#% cell
parametric_plot([[2cos(t), sin(t)], [cos(t)/2, 2sin(t)]], [t, 0, 2pi], legend: ["wide", "tall"])
#% output
#= image("parametric_plot.files/parametric_plot-3.svgz")

#% text
# A spiral that winds outwards:

#% cell
parametric_plot([t*cos(t), t*sin(t)], [t, 0, 6pi])
#% output
#= image("parametric_plot.files/parametric_plot-4.svgz")

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The path of a thrown stone with time as the parameter:

#% cell
parametric_plot([12t, 15t - 4.9t^2], [t, 0, 3], x_label: "distance (m)", y_label: "height (m)")
#% output
#= image("parametric_plot.files/parametric_plot-5.svgz")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `polar_plot`, `plot`, `list_line_plot`
